00:00.001  apparatus │ 
                      ── Plan: An ability opens a fight
                      
00:00.001  apparatus │   ~ The playtesting note of 2026-08-09, as a player would hit it. Two things were wrong and both
                      were invisible to the test suite, because every assertion about them passed:
                      
                        * The ability said "Your Kick takes effect!" — no target, no amount, no outcome. A player
                          could not tell a hit from a miss.
                        * A mob killed by an ability sat in the fight at zero health for ever. No experience, no
                          loot, no corpse, and the player was stuck Fighting: refused every later `kill`, and unable
                          to walk out of the room.
                      
                      Kick is the Warden's level-1 opener, so a fresh character can run this with no admin and no
                      levelling. That is deliberate: the plan guarding the most-reported bug should have the fewest
                      reasons not to run. The one thing it does need is something to kick, and it builds that itself.
                      
                      Whether the kick kills outright is a dice roll — it is 1.1x damage with a floor of 3, against a
                      city rat with barely more health than that. Both outcomes are worth having, so the plan waits for
                      the rat to fall rather than assuming which one killed it:
                      
                        * one-shot, and the closing half of the note is on display — experience, gold, and a fight that
                          ends;
                        * survived, and so is the opening half, because the Warden's own weapon carries on and finishes
                          it. A run where the kick rolled 9 instead of 10 caught exactly that: "Your Kick hits a rat for
                          9." and then, two seconds later, "You hit a rat for 2 damage. A rat falls."
                      
                      An earlier version assumed the one-shot and flagged four expectations the first time the dice
                      disagreed. `ability-then-melee` is the deterministic half — a dummy that reliably survives an
                      opener — so this one keeps waiting for the outcome rather than predicting it, which is the
                      honest shape for a plan about a real fight.
                      
                      The rat below is the one both this plan and `group-fight-ends` want, declared by both and built
                      once: fixtures are matched by key, so the second plan to ask finds what the first one made.
00:00.261  Theron   │   ~ registered as playtest_ysmevurj, playing Theron the Warden
00:00.338  Theron   │   *(info) The Reaches hold, Theron. Welcome back.
00:00.338  Theron   │   [hp 60/60 fp 20/20 sp 100/100 lvl 1 xp 0 gold 0]
00:00.345  Theron   │ The Gate Yard
                      The gate stands where it has always stood: a ring of dark stone twice the height of a man, set into the cobbles with no foundation and no seam. Nothing moves in it. Nobody living has seen anything move in it.
                      
                      Gatetown grew up around it anyway and treats it the way a town treats a large rock. Crates lean against the near side, children are told not to climb it, and the stone is worn shiny at shoulder height by three generations of people touching it on their way past.
                      
                      Yrriska's shrine sits at its foot — a bowl of coins, a knot of ribbon, and somebody's dice. It is the only untidy thing in the yard, and the sweepers go around it.
                      Exits: north, east, south, west
                      Hesper, who sweeps around the shrine is here.
